  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "The Strait of Hormuz is a critical global chokepoint, handling roughly 20–21 million barrels of crude oil and refined products per day, which represents approximately 25% of the world's seaborne oil trade. Over 80% of this oil is destined for Asian markets, primarily China, India, and Japan. It also transports roughly 20% of global LNG and one-third of global fertilizer trade. Key Details About Trade Through the Strait: Daily Volume: Approximately 20 million barrels per day (mb/d) of crude and petroleum products. Key Exporters: Saudi Arabia, UAE, Kuwait, Iraq, and Qatar rely on the strait, with roughly 80% of Gulf oil exports passing through. Alternative Routes: Limited options exist, such as the Abu Dhabi Crude Oil Pipeline (UAE) and the Petroline (Saudi Arabia). Global Impact: A complete closure of the Strait would remove 20% of global oil supply, significantly threatening global energy supplies and increasing prices. Key Findings: 20-21 Million barrels per day (2025/2026 data). ~25% of global seaborne oil trade. ~20% of global LNG and global oil supply.",
